\title{Coprimality, Counting, and a Fibonacci Bridge \\ in Stern's Diatomic Sequence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-02}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Stern's diatomic sequence $s$, defined by $s(0)=0$, $s(1)=1$, $s(2n)=s(n)$, and $s(2n+1)=s(n)+s(n+1)$, is a classical binary-recursive integer sequence whose values are controlled by the binary expansion of the index. We give a self-contained development of three exact results. First, consecutive values are always coprime: $\gcd(s(n),s(n+1))=1$ for all $n$; this is the arithmetic backbone of the sequence's role in enumerating the rationals. Second, the sequence \emph{counts} along the all-ones binary indices: $s(2^n-1)=n$, with the companion fixed-point identity $s(2^n)=1$ at pure powers of two. Third, and most strikingly, the sequence reproduces the even-index Fibonacci numbers along the Jacobsthal indices $J(n)=(4^n-1)/3$, whose binary expansions are alternating blocks of ones and zeros: $s(J(n))=F(2n)$, together with the odd-index companion $s(2J(n)+1)=F(2n+1)$. The Fibonacci bridge is established through a \emph{joint} two-term invariant showing that the coupled pair $(s(J(n)),\,s(2J(n)+1))$ satisfies exactly the same linear recurrence as $(F(2n),\,F(2n+1))$. We present the definitions, full proof sketches, algorithms, numerical demonstrations, and directions for further research.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Among the most economical objects in elementary number theory is \textbf{Stern's diatomic sequence} (also known as the Stern--Brocot ``fusc'' function). It is generated entirely by copying and adding, yet it encodes deep arithmetic structure: it parametrizes the Stern--Brocot tree, enumerates the nonnegative rationals in lowest terms, and interacts intimately with the base-two representation of its index.

This paper isolates three exact facts, each attached to a distinguished family of binary index patterns:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{All-index behavior (coprimality).} Neighboring values are coprime.
  \item \textbf{All-ones indices (counting).} $s(2^n-1)=n$.
  \item \textbf{Alternating-bit indices (Fibonacci bridge).} $s(J(n))=F(2n)$ where $J(n)=(4^n-1)/3$.
\end{itemize}
The unifying theme is that the binary expansion of the index dictates the arithmetic of the value, so the cleanest binary patterns (a single one, a block of ones, alternating ones and zeros) produce the cleanest arithmetic answers (the constant $1$, the counting numbers, and the Fibonacci numbers respectively). All results are proved by induction that mirrors the recursive construction of the sequence.

\section{Definitions and basic identities}

\begin{definition}[Stern's diatomic sequence]
Define $s:\N\to\N$ by
\[ s(0)=0,\qquad s(1)=1, \]
\[ s(2n)=s(n),\qquad s(2n+1)=s(n)+s(n+1)\quad(n\ge 0). \]
The recursion is well-founded because each recursive call strictly decreases the index, so $s$ is total and single-valued.
\end{definition}

The first terms are
\[ s = 0,1,1,2,1,3,2,3,1,4,3,5,2,5,3,4,1,5,4,7,\dots \]

\begin{lemma}[Equational form]
For all $n\ge 0$, $s(2n)=s(n)$ and $s(2n+1)=s(n)+s(n+1)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
These are the defining clauses with the parity condition discharged; they are the workhorses of every proof below.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity]
For all $n\ge 0$, $s(n+1)>0$; equivalently $s(m)=0$ iff $m=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $n$. If $n+1=2k$ with $k\ge 1$, then $s(2k)=s(k)>0$ by hypothesis. If $n+1=2k+1$, then $s(2k+1)=s(k)+s(k+1)\ge s(k+1)>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Coprimality of consecutive values}

\begin{theorem}[Coprime neighbors]
For every $n\ge 0$, $\gcd(s(n),\,s(n+1))=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $n$. The base case $\gcd(s(0),s(1))=\gcd(0,1)=1$ is immediate. Split on the parity of $n$.

If $n=2k$, then $(s(n),s(n+1))=(s(k),\,s(k)+s(k+1))$, and since $\gcd(a,a+b)=\gcd(a,b)$,
\[ \gcd(s(k),\,s(k)+s(k+1))=\gcd(s(k),s(k+1))=1 \]
by hypothesis (the case $k=0$ checked directly).

If $n=2k+1$, then $(s(n),s(n+1))=(s(k)+s(k+1),\,s(k+1))$, and since $\gcd(a+b,b)=\gcd(a,b)$,
\[ \gcd(s(k)+s(k+1),\,s(k+1))=\gcd(s(k),s(k+1))=1. \]
In both cases the new pair inherits coprimality from an earlier pair.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Symmetry]
$\gcd(s(n+1),\,s(n))=1$, since the greatest common divisor is symmetric.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Enumeration of the rationals]
The ratios $s(n)/s(n+1)$ traverse the nonnegative rationals, and the coprimality theorem guarantees each ratio is already in lowest terms, so no fraction is produced in reducible disguise. Coprimality is precisely the ingredient ensuring the enumeration has no duplicates.
\end{remark}

\section{Counting along the all-ones indices}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed points at powers of two]
For every $n\ge 0$, $s(2^n)=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. Base: $s(2^0)=s(1)=1$. Step: $2^{n+1}=2\cdot 2^n$ is even, so $s(2^{n+1})=s(2^n)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[All-ones indices count]
For every $n\ge 0$, $s(2^n-1)=n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. Base: $s(2^0-1)=s(0)=0$. Step: $2^{n+1}-1=2(2^n-1)+1$ is odd, so by the odd rule and the previous lemma,
\[ s(2^{n+1}-1)=s(2^n-1)+s(2^n)=n+1. \qedhere \]
\end{proof}

Interpreted in binary, $2^n-1$ is the string of $n$ ones, and the theorem says its Stern value equals the number of ones. Combined with the fixed-point lemma, the two simplest binary patterns produce the two simplest sequences: the constant $1$ and the counting numbers.

\section{The Stern--Fibonacci bridge}

Recall the Fibonacci sequence $F(0)=0$, $F(1)=1$, $F(k+2)=F(k+1)+F(k)$, and the \textbf{Jacobsthal numbers}
\[ J(n)=\frac{4^n-1}{3}=0,1,5,21,85,341,\dots \]

\begin{lemma}[Jacobsthal recurrence]
$J(n+1)=4J(n)+1$, and $3J(n)+1=4^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$4J(n)+1=(4^{n+1}-4)/3+1=(4^{n+1}-1)/3=J(n+1)$; the closed relation $3J(n)+1=4^n$ is the same identity cleared of the division, guaranteeing exactness with no division-by-zero corner case.
\end{proof}

In binary, $J(n)$ is the alternating block of $n$ ones interleaved with zeros; the recurrence prepends ``$01$''.

\begin{theorem}[Joint invariant]
For every $n\ge 0$, $s(J(n))=F(2n)$ and $s(2J(n)+1)=F(2n+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $a_n=s(J(n))$ and $b_n=s(2J(n)+1)$. Neither identity is provable in isolation, so we prove the pair simultaneously by induction on $n$.

\emph{Base.} $J(0)=0$, so $a_0=s(0)=0=F(0)$ and $b_0=s(1)=1=F(1)$.

\emph{Step.} Assume $a_n=F(2n)$, $b_n=F(2n+1)$. Using $J(n+1)=4J(n)+1$ and the equational lemmas,
\[ a_{n+1}=s(4J(n)+1)=s(2(2J(n))+1)=s(2J(n))+s(2J(n)+1)=a_n+b_n. \]
With $m=4J(n)+1=J(n+1)$ and $2J(n+1)+1=2m+1$,
\[ b_{n+1}=s(2m+1)=s(m)+s(m+1)=a_{n+1}+s(2(2J(n)+1))=a_{n+1}+b_n. \]
Hence
\[ a_{n+1}=a_n+b_n,\qquad b_{n+1}=a_{n+1}+b_n. \]
The even/odd-grouped Fibonacci numbers satisfy the same recurrence, $F(2n+2)=F(2n)+F(2n+1)$ and $F(2n+3)=F(2n+2)+F(2n+1)$, with matching seeds $(F(0),F(1))=(0,1)$. Therefore $a_n=F(2n)$ and $b_n=F(2n+1)$ for all $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stern--Fibonacci bridge]
For every $n\ge 0$, $s\!\left(\dfrac{4^n-1}{3}\right)=F(2n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from the joint invariant and $J(n)=(4^n-1)/3$, whose integrality is guaranteed by $3J(n)+1=4^n$.
\end{proof}

This is a genuine bridge between two classical integer sequences of entirely different origin --- a binary-recursive copy-and-add function and the additive Fibonacci recurrence --- which coincide exactly along an explicit sparse set of indices.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Direct recursive evaluation).} Evaluate $s(n)$ by the defining recursion with memoization; tabulating $s(0),\dots,s(N)$ costs $O(N)$.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Bottom-up tabulation).} Fill an array using $s(2k)=s(k)$ and $s(2k+1)=s(k)+s(k+1)$ in increasing index order; linear time and space.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Landmark evaluation).} Compute $s(2^n)=1$, $s(2^n-1)=n$, and $s(J(n))=F(2n)$ via the two-term recurrence $a_{n+1}=a_n+b_n$, $b_{n+1}=a_{n+1}+b_n$ in $O(n)$ additions, without materializing the exponentially large index $J(n)$.

\section{Numerical demonstrations}

Tabulating the first twenty values reproduces $0,1,1,2,1,3,2,3,1,4,3,5,2,5,3,4,1,5,4,7$. Checking $\gcd(s(n),s(n+1))$ over a large range returns $1$ every time. The all-ones indices give $s(2^n-1)=0,1,2,3,4,5,\dots$ and the powers of two give $s(2^n)=1$. The Jacobsthal landmarks give
\[ s(J(n))=0,1,3,8,21,55,144,377=F(2n),\quad s(2J(n)+1)=1,2,5,13,34,89,233,610=F(2n+1). \]

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\textbf{Dyadic row sums.} Numerically $\sum_{i=0}^{2^k-1}s(2^k+i)=3^k$; pairing each even index with its odd successor and telescoping collapses the level-$(k{+}1)$ sum to three times the level-$k$ sum.

\textbf{Extremal values.} The maximum of $s$ over $[2^k,2^{k+1})$ appears to equal $F(k+2)$, attained at the alternating-bit indices nearest the block ends.

\textbf{Enumeration ladder.} Coprimality shows each ratio $s(n)/s(n+1)$ is in lowest terms; conjecturally $n\mapsto s(n)/s(n+1)$ is a bijection onto the nonnegative rationals.

\textbf{Base-$b$ bridges.} Replacing the base-$4$ Jacobsthal indices by $(b^n-1)/(b-1)$ appears to yield, for each $b$, a linear divisibility sequence satisfying a second-order constant-coefficient recurrence depending only on $b$.

\section{Conclusion}

From a four-line copy-and-add rule we obtained three exact number-theoretic facts, each tied to a distinguished binary index pattern and proved by an induction mirroring the recursive definition. Stern's diatomic sequence thus serves as a compact laboratory in which coprimality, base-two structure, and the Fibonacci recurrence appear together and reinforce one another.

